        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "9999d30c-cb97-5de6-87ea-33594dd43f7a",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Works: Messiah Part II, Messiah (HWV 56)\n- People: George Frideric Handel, Jesus, Mary, Herod the Great\n- Texts: Gospel of Luke, Gospel of Matthew\n- Concepts: Nativity of Jesus, Messiah, annunciation to the shepherds\n\nFacts:\n- George Frideric Handel composed the English-language oratorio Messiah (HWV 56) in 1741, and the work is structured in three parts.\n- The listing titled Messiah Part II covers Part II in a table and comments on individual movements in relation to their text setting.\n- Part I of Messiah begins with the prophecy of the Messiah and his birth, includes the annunciation to the shepherds, and reflects the Messiah's deeds on earth.\n- The Nativity of Jesus, or birth of Jesus, is described in the Gospels of Luke and Matthew.\n- The accounts in Luke and Matthew agree that Jesus was born in Bethlehem during the time of Herod the Great to Mary, a betrothed virgin.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "1d41ce14-632b-5ce2-b8c5-de8e6816d596",
        "text": "Page: Messiah Part II. Sentence: Messiah (HWV 56), the English-language oratorio composed by George Frideric Handel in 1741, is structured in three parts.\nPage: Messiah Part II. Sentence: This listing covers Part II in a table and comments on individual movements, reflecting the relation of the musical setting to the text.\nPage: Messiah Part II. Sentence: Part I begins with the prophecy of the Messiah and his birth, shows the annunciation to the shepherds and reflects the Messiah's deeds on earth.\nPage: Nativity of Jesus. Sentence: The nativity of Jesus or birth of Jesus is described in the gospels of Luke and Matthew.\nPage: Nativity of Jesus. Sentence: The two accounts agree that Jesus was born in Bethlehem in the time of Herod the Great to a betrothed virgin whose name was Mary.",
